---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PersonaPickerWorkbench from "@/components/react/tools/persona-picker/PersonaPickerWorkbench";
import ServiceCTA from "@/components/ServiceCTA.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="AI Persona Picker | HunterMussel Tools"
  description="Browse 20 ready-made AI system prompt personas — Senior Engineer, DevOps, Code Reviewer, Technical Writer, and more. Copy any persona's system prompt with one click, or save your own to localStorage."
  canonicalPath="/tools/persona-picker/"
  keywords={[
    "ai persona picker",
    "system prompt templates",
    "ai persona library",
    "chatgpt system prompt",
    "claude system prompt",
    "llm persona generator",
    "ai role prompts",
    "system prompt examples",
    "ai assistant personas",
    "prompt engineering personas",
    "custom ai personas",
    "senior engineer prompt",
  ]}
>
  <main class="page pp-page">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">tool / ai</p>
      <h1>AI Persona Picker</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        20 hand-crafted system prompt personas for software engineers, DevOps, writers, and
        more. Browse by category, preview the system prompt, copy it with one click, and add
        your own custom personas — saved locally in your browser.
      </p>
      <a class="back-link" href="/tools/">← Back to tools</a>
    </section>

    <div class="card">
      <PersonaPickerWorkbench client:load />
    </div>

    <section class="article">
      <div class="info-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>What is a system prompt?</h3>
          <p>
            A system prompt is the instruction given to an AI model before the conversation starts.
            It sets the model's role, tone, constraints, and behavior — making it act as a
            Senior Engineer, Code Reviewer, or any other persona you need.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>How to use a persona</h3>
          <p>
            Click <strong>Copy prompt</strong> on any persona, then paste it as the system prompt
            in Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, or any other LLM interface. The model will adopt that
            persona for the conversation.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Custom personas</h3>
          <p>
            Click <strong>Add custom</strong> to create your own persona with a name, description,
            and system prompt. Custom personas are saved in your browser's localStorage — they
            persist across sessions and never leave your device.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <article class="card">
        <h2>Why bother with a persona at all?</h2>
        <p>
          "Act as a senior engineer" gets you a vague impression of one. A good persona prompt is
          specific: it names the role, the tone, what to prioritize, and what to push back on.
          That's the difference between an assistant that pads every answer with disclaimers and
          one that reviews your code like a colleague who's seen your stack before. The 20 personas
          here are written that way — pick one, paste it, and skip the ten minutes of prompt
          fiddling you'd otherwise repeat every session.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h2>Where these fit in real work</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Code review:</strong> load the Code Reviewer persona into a fresh chat before dropping in a diff — you get findings, not compliments.</li>
          <li><strong>Docs and writing:</strong> the Technical Writer persona keeps output structured and jargon-checked instead of generically enthusiastic.</li>
          <li><strong>Consistency with yourself:</strong> once you've tuned a prompt that works, save it as a custom persona so tomorrow-you starts from the same instructions as today-you.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h2>FAQ</h2>
        <h3>Do these work with any model?</h3>
        <p>Yes. A system prompt is plain text — paste it into Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, or a local model, anywhere that accepts a system or custom-instructions field.</p>
        <h3>Where are my custom personas stored?</h3>
        <p>In your browser's localStorage. They persist between visits on that device, never sync anywhere, and disappear if you clear site data — so keep a copy of prompts you care about.</p>
        <h3>Can I edit a built-in persona?</h3>
        <p>Copy its prompt, tweak the text, and save the result with <strong>Add custom</strong>. The built-ins stay untouched, and your variant sits alongside them.</p>
      </article>

      <ServiceCTA
        service="ai-automation"
        title="Need to build a custom AI assistant with a specific persona for your product?"
        description="HunterMussel designs and ships production AI agents with custom personas, RAG pipelines, and guardrails — tailored to your brand, audience, and use case."
        linkLabel="Explore AI Automation Services"
      />
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .pp-page {
    max-width: 900px;
    padding-bottom: 5rem;
  }
  .info-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(230px, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .info-card {
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid hsl(var(--border));
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    background: hsl(var(--card) / 0.5);
  }
  .info-card h3 {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    color: hsl(var(--foreground));
  }
  .info-card p {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: hsl(var(--muted-foreground));
    margin: 0;
  }
  .info-card strong {
    color: hsl(var(--foreground));
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
